 and mixed, with some surveys showing a tie but partisan-sponsored polls dominate", "Markets price Graham at roughly 85% while expert ratings cluster at Likely R"], "rationale": "The fundamentals favor Republicans in a state leaning R+8, and most raters call the race Likely R, but Andrews' fundraising advantage and polling averages showing a gap of only two to five points justify a Lean R call rather than Likely R. Prediction markets at about 14-17% for Andrews temper the optimism from the tightest surveys.", "watch": "A strong debate performance by Andrews or additional independent polling confirming a one-to-two-point race could shift this toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Darline Graham (R) is running."]}, "overview": "South Carolina's Senate race pits appointed incumbent Darline Graham against Democrat Annie Andrews in a state that leans Republican by eight points. Graham carries incumbency advantages and a Trump rally boost, but has raised little money herself, while outside groups have spent over a million dollars supporting her. Andrews has outraised her dramatically and remains on the air.\n\nPolling is thin and mixed: a Republican-sponsored September survey showed a two-point race, while an August live-caller poll showed Graham up thirteen. Averages land around a three-to-five-point Republican edge, and prediction markets price Andrews' chances in the mid-teens. Expert ratings mostly say Likely R, though Cook recently moved the race toward Democrats. The forecast expects Graham to win by a modest margin, with genuine uncertainty given the sparse polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1565, "margin": -5.15}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2537, "margin": -4.0}]}